Free the emulation coroutine and forget the loaded game in retro_deinit

retro_init creates the coroutine the emulator runs on (co_create, an 8 MB
stack) and nothing ever deletes it. retro_deinit also leaves
load_game_successful set.

A frontend that dlopens the core per session never notices either. Retro Pal
links the core statically and runs retro_init / retro_deinit once per game, so:
- every game played leaked the previous coroutine and whatever of its stack
  had been touched;
- after one successful game, a session whose retro_load_game was never
  reached still found load_game_successful set, and retro_deinit switched into
  a coroutine that had never been started.

retro_deinit now deletes the coroutine (the emulator has already switched back
out of it for the last time, at M64CMD_STOP) and clears the flag.

Applied at build time by Vendor/n64-ios/build.sh, after 0001. Worth sending
upstream.

--- a/libretro/libretro.c
+++ b/libretro/libretro.c
@@ -751,6 +751,19 @@
     CoreShutdown();
     deinit_audio_libretro();
 
+    /* Every retro_init creates the emulation coroutine; free it here, and
+     * forget the loaded game. A frontend that links the core statically and
+     * starts it again for the next game otherwise leaks the coroutine's stack
+     * each time, and carries load_game_successful into the next session, where
+     * a retro_deinit before any retro_load_game would switch into a coroutine
+     * that was never started. */
+    if (game_thread)
+    {
+        co_delete(game_thread);
+        game_thread = NULL;
+    }
+    load_game_successful = false;
+
     if (perf_cb.perf_log)
         perf_cb.perf_log();
 
